				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=348648&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606273926" alt="sirpudding" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=348648)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/sirpudding" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(348648); return false;" >sirpudding</a></span> <span class="odate time_1526562707 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">17 May 2018 13:11</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-3803325">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Agreed. The &quot;box&quot; does not necessarily mean a literal box (as in a locker, containment cell, etc.) The &quot;box&quot; is the containment procedure, and as long as the &quot;box&quot; isn't too fiddly, it's Safe. Feeding and cleaning up after them is part of the &quot;box&quot;. They aren't a separate procedure needed to keep the &quot;box&quot; working effectively.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yes, that's the box here. However Safe objects are supposed to be fine if you lock them in a box <em>and leave them alone indefinitely</em>. That second part doesn't seem to apply to this particular box.</p>
<p>One thing that object classes <em>could</em> be for is a way to categorically organize broad general policies. This would prevent the Foundation from wasting time and resources with a special case for everything, or worse, failing because trying to consistently apply thousands of specific policies is a recipe for disaster. I like a stricter intrepation of object classes for this reason.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;What&nbsp;do&nbsp;you&nbsp;mean?</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p><br />
If all safe objects are inertly sitting in hard vaults, all Euclid objects require ongoing but routine maintenance, and all Keter objects require high priority, difficult, or dynamic maintenance, then the Foundation can:</p>
<ul>
<li>Have $x/object budgeted for Safe containment based on the average locker in a vault, $nx/object dedicated to Euclid containment based on the average cost of a containment cell and for care and feeding, and object specific budgets for Keter containment plus a pessimistic estimate of what unknown new Keters might cost.</li>
<li>Have general policies for things like lockdowns, evacuation, and abandon-in-place. Being able to say that you can just lock up all the Safe objects is really useful here, and doesn't work if some Safes need to be evacuated or they'll die. Needing to reference and apply hundreds of special case instructions for every object at a facility is a terrible plan for an emergency.</li>
<li>Be able to design sites with future needs in mind and organize containment efficiently. Having some big vaults (for Safes) and cell blocks (for Euclids) behind multiple outer security layers is much better than having a random mix of different containment systems.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>That said there really isn't any consensus about this, and different authors do things differently. The only thing that matters is that more people like an article than hate it. I completely disagree with this being Safe, but not enough to downvote an otherwise great article, especially for what's essentially a procedural quibble about a wholly fictional bureaucratic process.</p>

							</div>

								
